{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he reached adulthood. During his early years his aunt Melisende of Tripoli also exercised some influence.\n\n*highlighted section part 1 Raymond IIIs early life and ascension*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and spent nearly ten years in prison. During his captivity his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch ruled Tripoli as regent. Raymond was released in 1174 after Nur ad-Din’s death. Upon his return he resumed control of Tripoli and also acted as regent for the young Bohemond IV of Antioch when Bohemond III died in 1187.\n\n*highlighted section part 2 Raymonds political role and captivity*\n\nRaymond III was involved in the succession disputes of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1174 he supported Miles of Plancy as regent for Baldwin IV but Miles was assassinated shortly after. Raymond then backed Raymond of Antioch as regent but Baldwin IV chose William of Montferrat instead. In 1185 Raymond helped crown Baldwin V as king of Jerusalem but Baldwin V died in 1186. Raymond initially opposed Guy of Lusignan’s claim to the throne but later accepted him after an agreement with Sibylla of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 3 Raymonds influence on Jerusalem*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly before the Battle of Hattin. His death left Tripoli in the hands of his cousin Bohemond IV. Raymond was known for his diplomatic skills and his efforts to maintain stability among the Crusader states during a turbulent period. His legacy includes his role in regional politics and his 